  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nI was testing some stuff and realized that in Firefox we don't actually create shadow roots when parsing `<template>` tags. I figure that behavior is probably unintended, but we don't really seem to have any tests for this.\n\nChrome on the other hand creates a #shadow-root.\n\nConsidering that template is not part of the default config, I don't think you should be able to create shadow roots unless you allow the `template` element?\n\nTest case:\n```\ndocument.body.setHTMLUnsafe(\"<div><template shadowrootmode='open' shadowrootdelegatesfocus><p>hello world</p></template></div>\" )\n```\n\nThis produces a `<div>` > `#shadow-root` > `<p>` tree in Chrome and just `<div>` in Firefox.\n\nOn the other hand, I feel like when using the most permissive sanitizer config `{}` Chrome's result makes sense.\n\nIn Firefox we always create:\n`<div>` > `<template>` (has a #document-fragment)\n\n@otherdaniel ",
